    "done_when": "A resumed run is never classified stalled on the quiet time of a stream from an earlier attempt. The classifier in reckon/crew/recovery.py measures a live run's quiet time from the later of two things: the current attempt's own launch time, and the last write to that attempt's own log. Before the current attempt's log exists, the run reads working within the same launch-window grace a fresh dispatch gets. Measured on 2026-09-26: watcher-seat-survives-other-hosts rendered complete → stalled (\"stream quiet for 956s\") at 20:26:34 local, 20 s after `crew resume`, because resume-1.jsonl did not exist yet and the classifier aged the run from the previous stream.jsonl. It recovered at 20:26:55. tests/test_resume_is_aged_from_its_own_start.py synthesises a live run record in tmp_path (the pytest fixture), with every crew directory environment-resolved to tmp_path and a live stub pid. Its stream.jsonl was last written 1000 s ago. It has just been resumed: the attempt was launched 5 s ago, and resume-1.jsonl is absent. The test asserts the classification is not stalled. A second case has the attempt's log last written 1000 s ago, and asserts stalled, so the guard still fires on a genuinely quiet resume. Negative control: age from the earliest stream again; the first case must fail. tests/test_crew_recovery.py adds no failures against the base, with pytest basetemp outside any git repository. Gate and control logs are written under the run directory. The change is committed in this worktree.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Make the classifier age a resumed run from its own attempt, never from an earlier attempt's stream.",
